using the python debugger in a notebook  in this video we'll learn how to use a  python debugger in a jupyter notebook or  a collab  for this example we're running code from  the token classification section  downloading the coinol dataset  looking a little bit at the data  before loading a tokenizer to preprocess  the world dataset  check out the section of the course  linked below for more information  once this is done  we try to load eight features from the  training dataset  and then batch them together using  tokenizer.pad  and we get the following error  we use pytorch here with written tensors  equal pt but you'll get the same error  with tensorflow  as we have seen in the how to debug an  error video the error message is at the  end of the traceback here it indexes us  we should use panning which we are  actually trying to do  so this is not useful at all and we'll  need to go a little deeper to debug the  problem  fortunately you can use the python  debugger at any time you get an error in  a jupyter notebook by typing the magic  command debug in a cell  don't forget the percent at the  beginning  when executing that cell you go to the  very bottom of the traceback where you  can type commands that will help you  debug your script  the first two commands you should learn  are u and d for up and down typing u and  enter  will take you up one step in the  traceback to the previous instruction  type d and then enter will take you one  step down in the traceback  going up twice we get to the point zero  was reached  the third command to learn for the  debugger is p for print  it allows you to print any value you  want  for instance  typing p return tensors and enter we see  the value pt that we pass to the pad  function  we can also have a look at the batch  output this patch encoding object gets  the battery output dictionary is a bit  hard to dig into  so let's dive into smaller pieces of it  inside the debugger you can not only  print any variable but also 